 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Storage Efficiency:** BiRF achieves extremely compact storage, with the small model variant (Ours-S2) requiring less than 0.5 MB, significantly outperforming baselines like TensoRF-CP (3.7 MB) and Instant-NGP (24.5 MB) on the "mic" scene while maintaining comparable or better PSNR [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On Synthetic-NeRF scenes, BiRF achieves a PSNR of 32.03 dB; on Synthetic-NSVF, 34.48 dB; and on Tanks and Temples, 28.20 dB [S2].
-*   **Training Time:** Training takes approximately 6, 9, and 14 minutes for Synthetic-NeRF scenes using Ours-B2 to Ours-B8 models on a single NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** The method provides a dramatic reduction in storage overhead compared to real-valued floating-point grids, requires no post-optimization, and uses a hybrid 2D-3D structure that mitigates hash collisions in 3D grids [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** The method is ideal for applications requiring extreme storage efficiency and fast deployment, such as mobile AR/VR or web-based 3D viewing, where model size is a critical constraint [S2].
+*   **Storage:** Extremely compact. Ours-S2 requires < 0.5 MB, outperforming TensoRF-CP (3.7 MB) and Instant-NGP (24.5 MB) on the mic scene [S2].
+*   **Training Time:** Approximately 6, 9, and 14 minutes for Synthetic-NeRF scenes using Ours-B2 to Ours-B8 models on a single NVIDIA RTX A6000 [S2].
+*   **Performance:** PSNR of 32.03 dB on Synthetic-NeRF, 34.48 dB on Synthetic-NSVF, and 28.20 dB on Tanks and Temples [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Significant reduction in storage overhead (1-bit parameters); no post-optimization required; competitive reconstruction quality.
+*   **Limitations:** Hash collisions in 3D grids at high resolutions can limit performance; mitigated by 2D grids but adds complexity [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When storage is a critical bottleneck and real-time or fast training is desired.
+*   **When not to use:** When maximum possible reconstruction quality is required over storage constraints, or when hardware does not support efficient hash table operations.
 
 ## Background
-Binary Radiance Fields were proposed by Seungjoo Shin and Jaesik Park from POSTECH and Seoul National University, published in arXiv:2306.07581v2 on December 4, 2023 [S2]. The method is implemented based on Instant-NGP, utilizing its multi-resolution hash encoding, and employs an occupancy grid from NerfAcc for efficient ray sampling [S2]. It addresses the storage burden imposed by implicit feature embeddings in modern NeRF variants by introducing binarization of feature embeddings to $\{-1, +1\}$ using a sign function and backpropagating through a straight-through estimator [S1]. The approach was implemented based on Instant-NGP using the occupancy grid from NerfAcc and trained on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs [S2].
+Binary Radiance Fields (BiRF) was proposed by Seungjoo Shin and Jaesik Park in 2023 [S2]. It builds on the Instant-NGP architecture [20] and NerfAcc [42] for efficient ray sampling. The method addresses the storage burden of implicit feature embeddings in NeRFs by introducing binarization during training [S1]. It is used for static scene reconstruction and novel-view synthesis [S2].
 
